    "q-181694": {
      "dom_id": "step-q181694",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14145534/assets/c670ed592fc32716596aae470911923627d1c6861a75a1c885659d09005e0a84.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36\\}.\n$$",
